  "outputText": "The user is asking me to explain why local inference benchmarks should report three specific metrics:\n1. Prompt prefill throughput\n2. Decode throughput\n3. Time to first token (TTFT)\n\nThis is a technical question about LLM inference performance measurement. Let me think deeply about what each of these means and why they matter, especially in a local (consumer/desktop) inference context.\n\nLet me break down the concepts:\n\n**Prefill vs Decode phases**\n\nModern LLM inference happens in two distinct phases:\n\n1. **Prefill phase**: When you send a prompt (input), the model processes all the tokens in the prompt simultaneously (in parallel) to compute the key-value (KV) cache. This is highly parallelizable work. The prompt is \"prefilled\" into the KV cache.\n\n2. **Decode phase**: The model generates output tokens one at a time. Each new token requires a forward pass through the model using the previous token. This is inherently sequential and memory-bandwidth-bound (the weights are loaded once per token generated).\n\n**Prompt Prefill Throughput**\n\nThis measures how fast the model can process the input prompt - typically measured in tokens per second (tok/s) for the input. \n\nWhy it matters:",
